<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Accessibility
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Naming the Group
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Screen readers can announce a name for the whole group. For example, set <Code>AriaLabel="Checkout progress"</Code>
    on <Code>Steps</Code>. To use an existing visible heading instead, set <Code>AriaLabelledBy</Code> to that heading's ID.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Keyboard Navigation
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Press <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> to reach the selected step, then use the left and right arrow keys to move between steps.
    <Kbd>Home</Kbd> and <Kbd>End</Kbd> move to the first and last steps. Press <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> or <Kbd>Space</Kbd>
    to open the chosen step, as long as your navigation rules allow it. Arrow keys alone do not change the displayed content.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Panel Focus
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    By default, pressing <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> after the selected step moves to the open panel, where users can read or scroll its content.
    Set <Code>Focusable="false"</Code> on <Code>StepPanel</Code> to skip the panel itself.
    Buttons, links, and form controls inside it remain reachable with <Kbd>Tab</Kbd>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Best Practices
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    True Sequence
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Steps suit a multi-stage task with a clear beginning and end, not general-purpose page navigation. Give each stage a short label that describes its goal and show the user's current position. Keep the sequence manageable by combining steps that are too small to stand on their own.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Protect Progress
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Validate the current step before allowing forward navigation, but let users return to completed steps without losing their input. If leaving the workflow would discard meaningful work, explain that consequence before navigating away.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Clear Labels
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use short, descriptive labels so users know what to expect at each step.
    Give the group a name that explains its purpose to screen reader users.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Keyboard Access
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Keep the focus highlight visible so users can see which step they are about to open.
    Keep panels reachable by keyboard when they contain introductory text or need scrolling.
    Skip the panel itself only when users can go straight to its controls without missing useful content.
</DocsPageParagraph>

